// vendor:enet — Dado bindings for the zpl-c/enet single-header fork of ENet
// (MIT; see LICENSE), the reliable-UDP library `core:net/enet` wraps.
//
//     import "vendor:enet"
//
//     enet.enet_initialize()
//     defer enet.enet_deinitialize()
//
//     ^enet.ENetAddress addr = enet.address_create()
//     defer enet.address_destroy(addr)
//     enet.address_set_any(addr)
//     enet.address_set_port(addr, 7777)
//     ^enet.ENetHost host = enet.enet_host_create(addr, 32, 2, 0, 0)
//     defer enet.enet_host_destroy(host)
//
// **Vendored IPv4-only — a deliberate choice, not upstream's default.**
// zpl-c/enet defaults to dual-stack IPv6 (`in6_addr` throughout); `enet.c`
// `#define`s `ENET_IPV4_ONLY` before including the header and the `foreign`
// block below asks for the same mode with `define "ENET_IPV4_ONLY=1"` (see
// `enet.c`'s own doc comment for the full reasoning — verified directly
// against raw BSD sockets that this project's own sandbox has no IPv6 at all,
// `EAFNOSUPPORT` on `socket(AF_INET6, …)`, which is common for minimal
// containers generally, not a one-off). `address_set_host` resolves IPv4
// literals and DNS names that resolve to one; an IPv6 literal (`"::1"`) will
// not resolve under this build. One macro, flipped in both places together,
// reverts to dual-stack if that is ever needed.
//
// **A `define` clause is program-global**: it is emitted above every
// `#include` in the single translation unit a Dado program is, so linking this
// package defines `ENET_IPV4_ONLY` for every package beside it. Nothing
// outside this directory names that macro, so it costs nothing today — but it
// is the property to check before adding a second `define` here.
//
// **Head start, hand-finished — not a raw binding-generator dump.** A first
// pass ran Dado's binding generator against the vendored `enet.h` (package
// `enet`, written to a scratch directory); it emitted 189 declarations clean
// and skipped 23. Reviewed against that draft (`bindgen: emitted 189
// declarations (20 enum, 35 type, 35 const, 99 fn); skipped 23`), this file
// keeps only the **public API** — the functions under `enet.h`'s own
// "Public API" banner (`enet_initialize` on) plus the five types a caller
// actually touches — and drops everything bindgen dutifully translated from
// ENet's *internal* protocol/command/list machinery (`ENetProtocol*`,
// `ENetChannel`, `ENetList`, `_ENetOutgoingCommand`, …): none of it is
// reachable from outside `enet.c`'s own translation unit, so a binding for
// it is dead surface a caller could misuse. The raw low-level socket
// primitives (`enet_socket_*`, `ENetSocketSet`/`fd_set`) are dropped for the
// same reason from the other direction — `core:net/enet` drives ENet through
// `ENetHost`, never the BSD socket underneath it.
//
// **Why bindgen skipped what it skipped, and why hand-authoring (not fixing
// `dadoc`) is the right response here.** Two `dadoc` limitations a
// transparent-struct restatement could hit were in play when this was
// written, and neither stands now:
//
//   * `ENetHost`, `ENetPeer`, `ENetChannel` all transitively reach
//     `ENetList`'s intrusive sentinel node — bindgen's note then: "depends on
//     unspellable type `_ENetList`" (`ENetList`'s own one-field
//     `sentinel: ENetListNode` hit the one-member collapse; a restatement
//     keeps its member list whole now). Opaque is still right: **every one of
//     these three is used only as an opaque handle by the public API** —
//     nothing outside `enet.c` walks a `ENetHost`'s peer list or a
//     `ENetChannel`'s window state directly, every value a caller needs is
//     already behind a getter (`enet_host_get_peers_count`,
//     `enet_peer_get_state`, …), and ENet's own header comment says as much
//     ("No fields should be modified unless otherwise specified" — `ENetPeer`).
//     `type ENetHost`/`ENetPeer` with no body — a foreign type declared with
//     no body is opaque, and only ever crosses by pointer — is
//     not a workaround for a `dadoc` bug here; it is the *correct* binding —
//     these were never meant to be poked at by value.
//   * `ENetEvent.type` and `ENetCompressor.context` were fields named with
//     Dado keywords. `context` is not one any more, and `@c("type") type_`
//     spells the other, as `sapp_event.type` and `sg_image_desc.type` are
//     spelled in `vendor:sokol`. `ENetCompressor` (custom
//     packet compression) is out of scope for this vendoring pass entirely —
//     it is not part of the networking baseline. `ENetEvent` **is** needed —
//     it is what `enet_host_service` fills in — and it is restated member for
//     member below, which is what `@c("type") type_` bought.
//   * `ENetAddress` carries a system address field `dadoc` had no declaration
//     for. It has one: the member is a `struct in_addr` under this binding's
//     own `ENET_IPV4_ONLY` mode, `InAddr` restates it above the generated
//     region, and the dual-stack `struct in6_addr` whose `s6_addr` glibc
//     `#define`s away is not the arm this package compiles.
//
// **`enet_shim.h`/`.c` are gone.** Fifteen hand-written C functions gave
// `ENetAddress` and `ENetEvent` a heap constructor and one accessor per member
// because neither could be restated; both are restated now, so the accessors
// are Dado one-liners over a member read. The accounting is in the block below
// the generated region.
//
// **What raises into a script: nothing.** Every declaration here is ENet's C,
// or a wrapper that passes a pointer to it, and the web's Dado stubs take and
// answer the same `rawptr`s, `cstring`s and `#c.*` values, so no function
// carries `#assert_raises`.
//
// **`size_t` parameters** (`enet_packet_create`'s `dataLength`,
// `enet_host_create`'s `peerCount`/`channelLimit`, …) are `#c.size_t`, C's own
// type, and not `u64`: the width is the same on every target Dado supports and
// the type is not — `u64` is `uint64_t`, which Darwin makes `unsigned long
// long` — and the emitter asserts each declaration's C type. A caller passes a
// literal as it is and converts a `u64` with `#c.size_t(n)`.
package enet

// bindgen:begin enums — regenerated; edits between the markers are lost
// bindgen:end enums

// The `enums` region above is empty and stays empty: a `foreign enum` has to
// be INSIDE the block, and that region sits outside it, so the enums are
// generated into the `declarations` region below with everything else.
//
// **The three enums are the generator's now, and they used to be hand-written
// here.** Twenty-one numbers were transcribed from `enet.h` by hand before
// CP-1, with a comment saying they had been checked, and nothing in the
// compiler, the gate or the C compiler ever compared one of them to the header
// again; CP-1 replaced them with a `foreign enum`, which declares the *names*
// and lets C supply every value, and CP-1b took the last hand-written step out
// by putting the names under the generator too. Nothing about the spellings
// moved: `bindgen.toml`'s `[types.rename]` and `[types.enum_members]` are
// where `EventType.Receive` is now said, and they are checked against the
// header on every regeneration — an enumerator ENet renames is a line in
// `scripts/bindings.py`'s output, where it used to be nothing at all.
//
// **`@c("…")` is what keeps both names.** `enet.h` spells these
// `ENET_EVENT_TYPE_*` / `ENET_PACKET_FLAG_*` / `ENET_PEER_STATE_*`; a caller
// wants to read `EventType.Receive`. The attribute on each member says which
// is which, so neither has to be given up — and the attribute on each `enum`
// says the same thing one level up, because the C types are
// `ENetEventType`, `ENetPacketFlag` and `ENetPeerState`.
//
// *(These are **not** anonymous C enums, which is what this comment used to
// say. `enet.h` writes `typedef enum _ENetPeerState { … } ENetPeerState;` and
// likewise for the other two — each is both tagged and typedef'd, which is
// exactly why `@c("ENetPeerState")` has a name to give and `#size(PeerState)`
// has something to ask `sizeof` about. libclang reports both declarations, and
// the typedef is the one bindgen emits: two Dado enums over one C type would be
// ERR1003.)*

// ── What the generated block below does not say ───────────────────────────
//
// The block is the binding generator's, between its markers, in `enet.h`'s own
// declaration order. These notes used to be interleaved with the declarations
// and a regeneration would have erased them, so they live out here now, which
// is where a binding's hand-written prose belongs:
//
//   * **What each `EventType` and `PacketFlag` member means**, keyed by the
//     member name so it is one `grep` from a line that uses one. These eleven
//     notes sat at the end of their own member lines until CP-1b put the enums
//     under the generator, which has no surface for a trailing comment — and
//     the honest answer was to move them here rather than build one. A rename
//     table is checked (an entry matching no enumerator is a report and a red
//     gate step); a comment table would be checked by nothing, so it would
//     carry the exact silent rot the rest of this config exists to end, and it
//     would want a fourth "matched nothing" report of its own to be worth
//     having. That is a lot of machinery for eleven lines in one binding of
//     eleven, and it would put a binding's prose in two places besides.
//
//       * `EventType.None` — no event within `enet_host_service`'s timeout.
//       * `EventType.Connect` — the `peer` field is the peer that connected.
//       * `EventType.Disconnect` — the `peer` field is the peer that
//         disconnected.
//       * `EventType.Receive` — the `peer`, `channelID` and `packet` fields
//         are all valid.
//       * `EventType.DisconnectTimeout` — the peer timed out rather than
//         disconnecting cleanly.
//       * `PacketFlag.Reliable` — resend until acknowledged, in order.
//       * `PacketFlag.Unsequenced` — not sequenced with other packets.
//       * `PacketFlag.NoAllocate` — the caller supplies (and owns) the data
//         buffer.
//       * `PacketFlag.UnreliableFragment` — fragment unreliably if it exceeds
//         the MTU.
//       * `PacketFlag.Unthrottled` — exempt from unreliable-send throttling.
//       * `PacketFlag.Sent` — read-only: set once every queue has sent it.
//
//     `PeerState`'s ten members carried no such note and still do not; the
//     name of each is the whole of what it says.
//   * **Three of the five `type` lines have no body on purpose.**
//     `ENetHost`, `ENetPeer` and `ENetPacket` are handles the public API only
//     ever passes by pointer; `bindgen.toml`'s `[types] opaque` is where that
//     is stated, and this file's own doc comment above says why for each one.
//     `ENetAddress` and `ENetEvent` are restated member for member, because a
//     caller fills one in and reads the other.
//   * **`enet_host_create`'s `address` may be nil** — that is what makes a
//     client host, one that binds no port and accepts no incoming connection.
//   * **`enet_host_check_events` and `enet_host_service` both fill `event`**,
//     which is reused across calls (see `event_create`), and both return >0
//     when an event landed, 0 when none did within `timeout`, and <0 on error.
//   * **`enet_peer_get_state`'s return is compared against `PeerState`** below,
//     and `event_type`'s against `EventType`, each with no conversion. `enet.h`
//     writes `ENetPeerState enet_peer_get_state(...)`, so `[types.rename]` gives
//     that return the Dado name `PeerState`; `event_type` is this package's own
//     wrapper and answers the member it reads, `ENetEvent.type_`, which is the
//     enum itself: `event_type(ev) == EventType.Receive`.
//   * **`enet_packet_create`'s `flags` is a bitwise-OR of `PacketFlag`**; a nil
//     `data` together with `NoAllocate` lets a caller fill the buffer itself
//     afterwards, via `enet_packet_get_data`.
//   * **The parameter names are `bindgen.toml`'s, not the header's.** `enet.h`
//     writes prototypes with types only, so a draft numbers them `a0, a1, …`;
//     the readable names are `[declarations.params]` in the config beside this
//     file, and bindgen refuses by name if one of those lists ever stops
//     matching its signature.
// ── `struct in_addr`, which differs by C library ─────────────────────────
//
// `ENetAddress.host` is a `struct in_addr` under `ENET_IPV4_ONLY`. POSIX makes
// it one member, `in_addr_t s_addr`. Winsock makes it a union, `S_un`, whose
// 32-bit arm is `u_long S_addr`, and `#define`s `s_addr` to `S_un.S_addr`: a
// restatement naming `s_addr` there comes out as `uint32_t S_un.S_addr;` in
// the emitter's shadow struct and does not compile. So each arm restates its
// own, the Windows one as the union's `u_long` arm under the union's own
// member name (the union's other two arms are the same four bytes), and each
// writes `address_set_any` over the member it has. These sit in a `foreign`
// block of their own, beside the generated one, so a regeneration leaves
// them alone.
when #OS == #WINDOWS:
    foreign "enet.h" define "ENET_IPV4_ONLY=1":
        type @c("struct in_addr") InAddr:
            (#c.ulong S_addr) S_un,

    // "Bind to every interface." `enet.h` spells this `ENET_HOST_ANY`, which under
    // `ENET_IPV4_ONLY` expands to its own `static const struct in_addr
    // enet_v4_anyaddr = { 0 }` — an address *value* rather than a scalar, which is
    // why it never crossed as a Dado const. The value it holds is one field wide and
    // is zero, and `host` is now a member this package can write, so the whole of
    // `enet_shim_address_set_any` is this line.
    void address_set_any(^ENetAddress addr): addr^.host.S_un.S_addr = 0
else when #OS != #WEB && #OS != #NONE:
    foreign "enet.h" define "ENET_IPV4_ONLY=1":
        // ── hand-written, above the marker, and it stays across a regeneration ──
        //
        // `ENetAddress.host` is a `struct in_addr`, which `<netinet/in.h>` declares
        // and `enet.h` only uses, so it is outside this binding's allowlist and
        // bindgen writes nothing for it — an `allow` entry matching no declaration
        // in `enet.h` is what `scripts/bindings.py` calls red. `bindgen.toml`'s
        // `[types.rename]` says `in_addr` is reached as `InAddr`, and this is the
        // declaration it is reached by.
        //
        // One member, `s_addr`, and it is an ordinary member rather than a macro —
        // which is the whole difference between this arm of `enet.h`'s `#ifdef` and
        // the other one, where `host` is a `struct in6_addr` whose `s6_addr` glibc
        // `#define`s to `__in6_u.__u6_addr8` and the emitter's shadow struct cannot
        // be written at all.
        type @c("struct in_addr") InAddr:
            u32 s_addr,

    // "Bind to every interface." `enet.h` spells this `ENET_HOST_ANY`, which under
    // `ENET_IPV4_ONLY` expands to its own `static const struct in_addr
    // enet_v4_anyaddr = { 0 }` — an address *value* rather than a scalar, which is
    // why it never crossed as a Dado const. The value it holds is one field wide and
    // is zero, and `host` is now a member this package can write, so the whole of
    // `enet_shim_address_set_any` is this line.
    void address_set_any(^ENetAddress addr): addr^.host.s_addr = 0

// ── the binding, where there is a C library to compile ENet with ───────────
//
// `#WEB` and `#NONE` have no `<stdlib.h>`, `<string.h>` or sockets, so neither
// `enet.c` nor these declarations can be compiled there (ERR1032). The `else`
// arm at the bottom declares the same names in Dado, so a package that imports
// this one still builds for a page or for bare metal, and every function there
// fails the way ENet fails when it cannot start.
when #OS != #WEB && #OS != #NONE:
    foreign "enet.h" define "ENET_IPV4_ONLY=1":
        // bindgen:begin declarations — regenerated; edits between the markers are lost
        // ── Enumerations (values supplied by C) ───────────────────────────────
        enum @c("ENetPacketFlag") PacketFlag:
            @c("ENET_PACKET_FLAG_RELIABLE") Reliable,
            @c("ENET_PACKET_FLAG_UNSEQUENCED") Unsequenced,
            @c("ENET_PACKET_FLAG_NO_ALLOCATE") NoAllocate,
            @c("ENET_PACKET_FLAG_UNRELIABLE_FRAGMENT") UnreliableFragment,
            @c("ENET_PACKET_FLAG_UNTHROTTLED") Unthrottled,
            @c("ENET_PACKET_FLAG_SENT") Sent,

        enum @c("ENetPeerState") PeerState:
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_DISCONNECTED") Disconnected,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_CONNECTING") Connecting,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_ACKNOWLEDGING_CONNECT") AcknowledgingConnect,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_CONNECTION_PENDING") ConnectionPending,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_CONNECTION_SUCCEEDED") ConnectionSucceeded,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_CONNECTED") Connected,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_DISCONNECT_LATER") DisconnectLater,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_DISCONNECTING") Disconnecting,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_ACKNOWLEDGING_DISCONNECT") AcknowledgingDisconnect,
            @c("ENET_PEER_STATE_ZOMBIE") Zombie,

        enum @c("ENetEventType") EventType:
            @c("ENET_EVENT_TYPE_NONE") None,
            @c("ENET_EVENT_TYPE_CONNECT") Connect,
            @c("ENET_EVENT_TYPE_DISCONNECT") Disconnect,
            @c("ENET_EVENT_TYPE_RECEIVE") Receive,
            @c("ENET_EVENT_TYPE_DISCONNECT_TIMEOUT") DisconnectTimeout,

        // ── Types ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        type ENetPacket
        type ENetAddress:
            InAddr host,
            u16    port,
            u16    sin6_scope_id,

        type ENetPeer
        type ENetHost
        type ENetEvent:
            EventType @c("type") type_,
            ^ENetPeer            peer,
            u8                   channelID,
            u32                  data,
            ^ENetPacket          packet,

        // ── Functions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        ^ENetPacket enet_packet_create(const rawptr data, #c.size_t dataLength, u32 flags)
        void enet_packet_destroy(^ENetPacket packet)
        i32 enet_initialize()
        void enet_deinitialize()
        u32 enet_linked_version()
        u32 enet_time_get()
        i32 enet_address_set_host_new(^ENetAddress address, cstring hostName)
        i32 enet_address_get_host_new(^const ENetAddress address, ^#c.char hostName, #c.size_t nameLength)
        u32 enet_host_get_peers_count(^ENetHost host)
        u32 enet_host_get_packets_sent(^ENetHost host)
        u32 enet_host_get_packets_received(^ENetHost host)
        u32 enet_host_get_bytes_sent(^ENetHost host)
        u32 enet_host_get_bytes_received(^ENetHost host)
        u32 enet_peer_get_rtt(^ENetPeer peer)
        PeerState enet_peer_get_state(^ENetPeer peer)
        rawptr enet_peer_get_data(^ENetPeer peer)
        void enet_peer_set_data(^ENetPeer peer, const rawptr data)
        rawptr enet_packet_get_data(^ENetPacket packet)
        u32 enet_packet_get_length(^ENetPacket packet)
        ^ENetHost enet_host_create(^const ENetAddress address, #c.size_t peerCount, #c.size_t channelLimit, u32 incomingBandwidth, u32 outgoingBandwidth)
        void enet_host_destroy(^ENetHost host)
        ^ENetPeer enet_host_connect(^ENetHost host, ^const ENetAddress address, #c.size_t channelCount, u32 data)
        i32 enet_host_check_events(^ENetHost host, ^ENetEvent event)
        i32 enet_host_service(^ENetHost host, ^ENetEvent event, u32 timeout)
        void enet_host_flush(^ENetHost host)
        void enet_host_broadcast(^ENetHost host, u8 channelID, ^ENetPacket packet)
        void enet_host_channel_limit(^ENetHost host, #c.size_t channelLimit)
        void enet_host_bandwidth_limit(^ENetHost host, u32 incomingBandwidth, u32 outgoingBandwidth)
        i32 enet_peer_send(^ENetPeer peer, u8 channelID, ^ENetPacket packet)
        ^ENetPacket enet_peer_receive(^ENetPeer peer, ^u8 channelID)
        void enet_peer_ping(^ENetPeer peer)
        void enet_peer_reset(^ENetPeer peer)
        void enet_peer_disconnect(^ENetPeer peer, u32 data)
        void enet_peer_disconnect_now(^ENetPeer peer, u32 data)
        void enet_peer_disconnect_later(^ENetPeer peer, u32 data)

    // bindgen: emitted 43 declarations (3 enum, 5 type, 0 const, 35 fn); skipped 0; 153 declaration(s) the config's allowlist does not ask for.
    // bindgen:end declarations

    // ── What used to be `enet_shim.{h,c}` ─────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Fifteen hand-written C functions stood here — a heap constructor and
    // destructor for `ENetAddress` and for `ENetEvent`, one getter or setter per
    // member either of them exposed, and one `memcpy`. Every one of them is now
    // Dado, because both structs are restated transparently above and a member of a
    // transparent restatement is reached with `.`.
    //
    // **The stated blocker was a macro and it was not this package's.** The shim
    // said `ENetAddress` could not be restated because glibc writes
    // `#define s6_addr __in6_u.__u6_addr8`, and the emitter's layout check writes a
    // shadow struct carrying each member's C name, so the shadow came out as
    // `uint8_t __in6_u.__u6_addr8[16];` and no spelling of `@c` could rescue it —
    // `@c` is what *supplies* the eaten name. All of that is true and none of it
    // applies here: `enet.c` compiles with `ENET_IPV4_ONLY`, under which
    // `ENetAddress.host` is a `struct in_addr` whose `s_addr` is an ordinary
    // member, and the `define "ENET_IPV4_ONLY=1"` clause on the `foreign` line
    // above puts this translation unit in the same mode. There were three
    // translation units, two agreeing and one not, and the third had no way to say
    // so until a `define` clause existed.
    //
    // **A `define` clause is program-global**: it is emitted above every `#include`
    // in the one translation unit a program is, so `ENET_IPV4_ONLY` is defined for
    // every package linked beside this one. That is correct and intended for this
    // macro — it is ENet's own name, ENet is the only thing that reads it, and the
    // alternative is the disagreement above. It is worth knowing before a second
    // `define` is added for a name something else might also read.
    //
    // The three functions below are libc's, restated the way `core:mem/libc`
    // restates `malloc` — `size_t` as `#c.size_t`, which is its type on glibc and
    // on Darwin, where `u64` is `unsigned long long` and the emitter's `_Generic`
    // assertion refuses it. `calloc` rather than `malloc` because an address and an
    // event are both handed to ENet expecting to be zeroed, which is what the C
    // these replace did. `memcpy` is `@macro` for `app:ui/grid`'s `memmove`'s
    // reason: Darwin's `<string.h>` fortifies it into a macro, and an unmarked
    // binding of a macro is a build error there.
    private foreign "stdlib.h":
        rawptr calloc(#c.size_t count, #c.size_t size)
        void free(rawptr ptr)

    private foreign "string.h":
        @macro rawptr memcpy(rawptr dst, const rawptr src, #c.size_t n)

    // `rawptr` converts to and from any `^T` through a written conversion, and a
    // conversion is spelled with a type's name — so the two pointer types a
    // constructor hands back are named here rather than written inline.
    private type AddressPtr: ^ENetAddress
    private type EventPtr: ^ENetEvent

    // `enet_address_get_host_new` writes into a `char *`, which the generated
    // declaration says (`^#c.char`); `address_get_host` hands it the first byte
    // of the caller's buffer, converted here.
    private type HostNameBuf: ^#c.char

    // A zeroed `ENetAddress` from C's heap, for the calls that take one by
    // pointer; end it with `address_destroy`. Dado cannot hand back a pointer to
    // a stack value that outlives the call, so the address lives on the heap.
    // `nil` when the allocation fails, which is `calloc`'s own answer.
    ^ENetAddress address_create():
        return AddressPtr(calloc(1, #c.size_t(#size(ENetAddress))))

    // Give back an address `address_create` made.
    void address_destroy(^ENetAddress addr): free(rawptr(addr))

    // Set `addr`'s host to `hostname`, an IPv4 literal or a name ENet resolves
    // (blocking, IPv4 only). `false` when it does not resolve.
    bool address_set_host(^ENetAddress addr, cstring hostname):
        return enet_address_set_host_new(addr, hostname) == 0

    // Set `addr`'s port.
    void address_set_port(^ENetAddress addr, u16 port): addr^.port = port

    // `addr`'s port.
    u16 address_get_port(^ENetAddress addr): return addr^.port

    // Write `addr`'s host into `out` as NUL-terminated text: its name when a
    // reverse lookup finds one, else its dotted quad. `false` when `out` is
    // empty or too short for it.
    bool address_get_host(^ENetAddress addr, []char8 out):
        if #len(out) == 0: return false
        return enet_address_get_host_new(addr, HostNameBuf(rawptr(&out[0])), #c.size_t(#len(out))) == 0

    // A zeroed `ENetEvent` from C's heap, for `enet_host_service` to fill; end
    // it with `event_destroy`. `nil` when the allocation fails.
    ^ENetEvent event_create():
        return EventPtr(calloc(1, #c.size_t(#size(ENetEvent))))

    // Give back an event `event_create` made.
    void event_destroy(^ENetEvent ev): free(rawptr(ev))

    // What the event `ev` reports.
    EventType event_type(^ENetEvent ev): return ev^.type_

    // The peer the event `ev` happened to.
    ^ENetPeer event_peer(^ENetEvent ev): return ev^.peer

    // The channel a `Receive` event's packet arrived on.
    u8 event_channel_id(^ENetEvent ev): return ev^.channelID

    // The data a `Connect` or `Disconnect` event carries (the peer's `data`
    // argument).
    u32 event_data(^ENetEvent ev): return ev^.data

    // A `Receive` event's packet, which the caller destroys
    // (`enet_packet_destroy`).
    ^ENetPacket event_packet(^ENetEvent ev): return ev^.packet

    // Copy a received packet's bytes into `out`, as many as fit, and answer how
    // many that was: the packet's length, or `#len(out)` when `out` is shorter.
    // `ENetPacket` stays opaque, so the length and the data come from `enet.h`'s
    // own getters; the copy is `memcpy`, an ordinary extern.
    u64 packet_copy(^ENetPacket packet, []u8 out):
        u64 n = u64(enet_packet_get_length(packet))
        if n > u64(#len(out)): n = u64(#len(out))
        if n > 0:
            _ = memcpy(rawptr(&out[0]), enet_packet_get_data(packet), #c.size_t(n))
        return n
else:
    // `#WEB` and `#NONE`: the same names, in Dado, and nothing to drive. A page
    // has no UDP socket and bare metal has no network stack, so
    // `enet_initialize` fails (-1) and every constructor answers nil; a caller
    // that checks either, as `core:net/enet` does, never reaches the rest. The
    // rest answers what ENet answers for a call it cannot carry out: a
    // negative status, zero, or nil. The three enums carry `enet.h`'s values.
    enum i32 PacketFlag:
        Reliable           = 1,
        Unsequenced        = 2,
        NoAllocate         = 4,
        UnreliableFragment = 8,
        Unthrottled        = 16,
        Sent               = 256,

    enum i32 PeerState:
        Disconnected,
        Connecting,
        AcknowledgingConnect,
        ConnectionPending,
        ConnectionSucceeded,
        Connected,
        DisconnectLater,
        Disconnecting,
        AcknowledgingDisconnect,
        Zombie,

    enum i32 EventType:
        None,
        Connect,
        Disconnect,
        Receive,
        DisconnectTimeout,

    // The handles are never built here, so their bodies are a placeholder
    // byte; a pointer to one is always nil.
    type ENetPacket:
        u8 unused,

    type ENetPeer:
        u8 unused,

    type ENetHost:
        u8 unused,

    type InAddr:
        u32 s_addr,

    type ENetAddress:
        InAddr host,
        u16    port,
        u16    sin6_scope_id,

    type ENetEvent:
        EventType   type_,
        ^ENetPeer   peer,
        u8          channelID,
        u32         data,
        ^ENetPacket packet,

    ^ENetPacket enet_packet_create(const rawptr data, #c.size_t dataLength, u32 flags):
        _ = data
        _ = dataLength
        _ = flags
        return nil

    void enet_packet_destroy(^ENetPacket packet): _ = packet
    i32 enet_initialize(): return -1
    void enet_deinitialize(): return
    u32 enet_linked_version(): return 0
    u32 enet_time_get(): return 0
    i32 enet_address_set_host_new(^ENetAddress address, cstring hostName):
        _ = address
        _ = hostName
        return -1

    i32 enet_address_get_host_new(^const ENetAddress address, ^#c.char hostName, #c.size_t nameLength):
        _ = address
        _ = hostName
        _ = nameLength
        return -1

    u32 enet_host_get_peers_count(^ENetHost host):
        _ = host
        return 0

    u32 enet_host_get_packets_sent(^ENetHost host):
        _ = host
        return 0

    u32 enet_host_get_packets_received(^ENetHost host):
        _ = host
        return 0

    u32 enet_host_get_bytes_sent(^ENetHost host):
        _ = host
        return 0

    u32 enet_host_get_bytes_received(^ENetHost host):
        _ = host
        return 0

    u32 enet_peer_get_rtt(^ENetPeer peer):
        _ = peer
        return 0

    PeerState enet_peer_get_state(^ENetPeer peer):
        _ = peer
        return PeerState.Disconnected

    rawptr enet_peer_get_data(^ENetPeer peer):
        _ = peer
        return nil

    void enet_peer_set_data(^ENetPeer peer, const rawptr data):
        _ = peer
        _ = data

    rawptr enet_packet_get_data(^ENetPacket packet):
        _ = packet
        return nil

    u32 enet_packet_get_length(^ENetPacket packet):
        _ = packet
        return 0

    ^ENetHost enet_host_create(^const ENetAddress address, #c.size_t peerCount, #c.size_t channelLimit, u32 incomingBandwidth, u32 outgoingBandwidth):
        _ = address
        _ = peerCount
        _ = channelLimit
        _ = incomingBandwidth
        _ = outgoingBandwidth
        return nil

    void enet_host_destroy(^ENetHost host): _ = host
    ^ENetPeer enet_host_connect(^ENetHost host, ^const ENetAddress address, #c.size_t channelCount, u32 data):
        _ = host
        _ = address
        _ = channelCount
        _ = data
        return nil

    i32 enet_host_check_events(^ENetHost host, ^ENetEvent event):
        _ = host
        _ = event
        return -1

    i32 enet_host_service(^ENetHost host, ^ENetEvent event, u32 timeout):
        _ = host
        _ = event
        _ = timeout
        return -1

    void enet_host_flush(^ENetHost host): _ = host
    void enet_host_broadcast(^ENetHost host, u8 channelID, ^ENetPacket packet):
        _ = host
        _ = channelID
        _ = packet

    void enet_host_channel_limit(^ENetHost host, #c.size_t channelLimit):
        _ = host
        _ = channelLimit

    void enet_host_bandwidth_limit(^ENetHost host, u32 incomingBandwidth, u32 outgoingBandwidth):
        _ = host
        _ = incomingBandwidth
        _ = outgoingBandwidth

    i32 enet_peer_send(^ENetPeer peer, u8 channelID, ^ENetPacket packet):
        _ = peer
        _ = channelID
        _ = packet
        return -1

    ^ENetPacket enet_peer_receive(^ENetPeer peer, ^u8 channelID):
        _ = peer
        _ = channelID
        return nil

    void enet_peer_ping(^ENetPeer peer): _ = peer
    void enet_peer_reset(^ENetPeer peer): _ = peer
    void enet_peer_disconnect(^ENetPeer peer, u32 data):
        _ = peer
        _ = data

    void enet_peer_disconnect_now(^ENetPeer peer, u32 data):
        _ = peer
        _ = data

    void enet_peer_disconnect_later(^ENetPeer peer, u32 data):
        _ = peer
        _ = data

    // The wrappers above the stubs, with nothing to allocate or copy.
    ^ENetAddress address_create(): return nil
    void address_destroy(^ENetAddress addr): _ = addr
    bool address_set_host(^ENetAddress addr, cstring hostname):
        _ = addr
        _ = hostname
        return false

    void address_set_any(^ENetAddress addr):
        if addr != nil: addr^.host.s_addr = 0

    void address_set_port(^ENetAddress addr, u16 port):
        if addr != nil: addr^.port = port

    u16 address_get_port(^ENetAddress addr):
        if addr == nil: return 0
        return addr^.port

    bool address_get_host(^ENetAddress addr, []char8 out):
        _ = addr
        _ = out
        return false

    ^ENetEvent event_create(): return nil
    void event_destroy(^ENetEvent ev): _ = ev
    EventType event_type(^ENetEvent ev):
        if ev == nil: return EventType.None
        return ev^.type_

    ^ENetPeer event_peer(^ENetEvent ev):
        if ev == nil: return nil
        return ev^.peer

    u8 event_channel_id(^ENetEvent ev):
        if ev == nil: return 0
        return ev^.channelID

    u32 event_data(^ENetEvent ev):
        if ev == nil: return 0
        return ev^.data

    ^ENetPacket event_packet(^ENetEvent ev):
        if ev == nil: return nil
        return ev^.packet

    u64 packet_copy(^ENetPacket packet, []u8 out):
        _ = packet
        _ = out
        return 0

// What ENet costs at the link step, as a `link` clause on the declaration
// rather than a sidecar file.
//
// Nothing on Linux or macOS: ENet's socket layer is plain BSD sockets
// (`socket`, `bind`, `connect`, `select`, `getaddrinfo`, …) plus
// `<netinet/in.h>`'s `in6_addr`/`in6addr_any`, all in libc and libSystem
// respectively. Nothing here is an external library the way sokol_gfx's
// D3D11/Metal/GL backends are.
//
// On Windows ENet's socket layer is Winsock: `ws2_32` for the sockets and
// `winmm` for `timeGetTime`, which `enet_time_get` calls there.
// `scripts/wintest.sh collections/vendor/enet` cross-compiles and links the
// test against these two for `x86_64-windows-gnu`, which is what confirms the
// list; the test has not been run on Windows.
//
// A `when` over `#OS` folds, so the clause is read only by a Windows build.
when #OS == #WINDOWS:
    foreign "enet.h" link "-lws2_32 -lwinmm"
